# Mirrors the ignore set in eslint.config.mjs so the standalone `prettier` CLI
# and `eslint` (which runs prettier via the prettier/prettier rule) agree on
# exactly which files they format. Keep the two lists in sync when either moves.
#
# NB: prettier's own formatting options live in .prettierrc.json, which is now
# the SINGLE source of truth — eslint.config.mjs passes no options to the
# `prettier/prettier` rule, so the plugin resolves that same file. Without it
# the CLI silently fell back to its defaults (2-space, 80 cols), disagreed with
# eslint on ~60 point_of_sale files alone, and reformatting with it produced
# pure churn that contradicted the repo style.

# Asset-pipeline test fixtures. These are deliberately odd *inputs* to the
# SCSS/CSS compiler, so normalising them defeats their purpose: test_error.scss
# is invalid on purpose (`.rule1 ()){`) to exercise the compile-error path, and
# test_split_marker.scss carries a `/*! <hex> */` loud comment of the exact
# shape that once broke rx_css_split. A formatter must not reshape the syntax
# these exist to guard.
#
# Scoped to the fixture roots, NOT to static/**. The broad form also swept in
# static/tests/**, which is ordinary hand-written code (a Hoot suite, a tour, a
# lazy-loaded component) with no reason to be exempt -- and because eslint runs
# prettier through the prettier/prettier rule, which honours THIS file, those
# files were correctness-linted but silently formatting-exempt. eslint's own
# list ignores only static/src/js/*.js; that asymmetry is what the header above
# means by keeping the two in sync.
odoo/addons/test_assetsbundle/static/src/**
odoo/addons/test_assetsbundle/static/invalid_src/**

# Vendored third-party libraries (not our code). By convention vendored code
# lives under <module>/static/lib and nowhere else.
#
# Written as `/**/*` (files) rather than `/**` (files AND the directory), for the
# same reason as eslint.config.mjs: a pattern that ignores the directory prunes
# the walk, and the negation below can then re-include nothing. Ignoring only the
# files keeps it effective.
**/static/lib/**/*
# hoot is first-party despite living under web/static/lib (historical). The
# directory itself has to be un-ignored too, or its contents cannot be
# re-included: gitignore semantics refuse to resurrect a file whose parent
# directory is excluded.
!addons/web/static/lib/hoot
!addons/web/static/lib/hoot/**
# Vendored bundle that predates the convention.
addons/spreadsheet/static/src/o_spreadsheet/o_spreadsheet.js
# Vendored from the "content-disposition" npm package; it carries a file-level
# `/* eslint-disable */` instead of living under static/lib, and eslint honours
# that comment while the prettier CLI cannot see it. Without this line the two
# gates disagree: `eslint` exits 0 while `prettier --check` exits 1.
addons/web/static/src/core/network/content_disposition.js
# Same mismatch, but first-party: this file carries a mid-file
# `/* eslint-disable */` (line 33) to keep its property lists hand-formatted
# one entry per line, which eslint honours and the CLI cannot see. Listed so
# the two gates agree. Unlike the vendored cases above, the blanket disable
# here is worth revisiting -- it silences every rule for the rest of the file
# just to pin formatting.
addons/mass_mailing/static/src/builder/plugins/customize_mailing_variables.js
# Generated emoji table (~546 KB, one enormous array literal). The prettier CLI
# cannot format it at all -- it dies with "RangeError: Maximum call stack size
# exceeded" walking the AST, which made `prettier --check addons/web` exit 2
# regardless of any option. eslint's prettier/prettier rule still covers it, so
# this only spares the CLI a crash it cannot recover from.
addons/web/static/src/components/emoji_picker/emoji_data.js

